---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { BRAND, legalStatusLabels, legalStatusHelp } from '../lib/labels';
import { withBase } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description={`How ${BRAND.name} selects cases, cites sources, and labels legal status.`}
>
  <div class="container-narrow pt-10 sm:pt-14 pb-16 prose-case">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent mb-3">
      {BRAND.descriptor}
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl mb-4">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-10">
      This site documents significant Bitcoin-related criminal cases without
      inventing facts. Accuracy is a publication rule: claims without sources do
      not belong in a case file.
    </p>

    <h2>What we include</h2>
    <p>A case is eligible only if all of the following hold:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Bitcoin-primary</strong> — Bitcoin is central to the offense,
        proceeds, payment, or seizure (not a trivial side rail).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public record</strong> — Core claims rest on public primary
        documentation: court filings, judgments, or competent agency releases
        (for example U.S. Department of Justice or U.S. Attorney’s Offices).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Significance</strong> — Historically or financially important
        enough to warrant a curated case file. Current coverage focuses on
        high-profile matters.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we exclude</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Unverified social-media allegations</li>
      <li>On-chain “labels” without an authority source</li>
      <li>Pure altcoin scams outside Bitcoin-primary scope</li>
      <li>Speculation about uncharged parties without public record</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Primary</strong> — Court documents; agency press releases and
        affidavits; blockchain facts only when framed by a competent source
        (for example a seizure amount announced by DOJ).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Secondary</strong> — Reputable news or academic work for
        context, not as the sole support for guilt or amounts when a primary
        source exists.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Every published case requires at least one primary source. High-confidence
      files require a primary court, agency, or blockchain-attested source of
      those kinds.
    </p>

    <h2>Money and numbers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Prefer <strong>BTC</strong> amounts as reported by the source.</li>
      <li>
        Any <strong>USD</strong> figure includes an as-of date and a citation.
        Dollar values change with price; undated USD claims are not published.
      </li>
      <li>
        Approximate figures are labeled the way the source labels them (e.g.
        “approximately”).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Legal status language</h2>
    <p>We label process status explicitly:</p>
    <ul>
      {
        (
          Object.keys(legalStatusLabels) as (keyof typeof legalStatusLabels)[]
        ).map((key) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{legalStatusLabels[key]}</strong> — {legalStatusHelp[key]}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Case lifecycle (open / closed / partial) is separate: it describes whether
      the threads we document are finished, not whether someone is guilty.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial voice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Prefer “according to [source]”, “the jury convicted”, “pled guilty to”,
        “the government alleged”.
      </li>
      <li>
        Avoid sensational labels (“kingpin”, “mastermind”) unless quoting a
        source.
      </li>
      <li>
        When sources conflict, state both and lower confidence rather than
        picking a dramatic number.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Geographic and institutional bias</h2>
    <p>
      Current coverage draws heavily on U.S. federal public records. That does
      not mean crime is U.S.-only; it means our inclusion bar is easiest to meet
      where English-language agency releases and court dockets are public. We
      aim to widen coverage without lowering the source standard.
    </p>

    <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Each case shows a <strong>last reviewed</strong> date. Material
      corrections (appeals, sentence changes, new seizures) should update that
      field and the structured timeline. See
      <a href={withBase('/about#corrections')}>corrections policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not comprehensive; many crimes are never public or never prosecuted.</li>
      <li>Not a live blockchain scanner or address-risk tool.</li>
      <li>Not legal advice; not affiliated with any law-enforcement agency.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How publication is checked</h2>
    <p>
      Case metadata is validated when the site is built: every amount must point
      at a listed source; every USD amount needs a date; every high-confidence
      file needs a strong primary source. That process is internal quality
      control — readers should still open the cited links.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
